course: dictionary
lesson: 11368
title: Abdicate: giving up a crown or a duty
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Abdicate is a hard word, but its clues are helpful. «The old queen decided to abdicate, so her son became the new ruler.» The clues are the queen deciding, and her son becoming ruler. So abdicate means to give up being king or queen.
explain: You can also abdicate a duty. «The captain abdicated his responsibility and left the crew to cope alone.» Here the captain did not do what he should have done. Abdicate can mean failing to take on a job that belongs to you.
explain: Test your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «The queen gave up her throne» still makes sense, so you have found the right meaning. Check your guess this way every time you meet a new word.
fact: Abdicate means to give up being king or queen.
fact: Abdicate is a verb: abdicate, abdicates, abdicated, abdicating.
fact: In 1936, King Edward VIII abdicated the British throne.
fact: To abdicate a responsibility means to fail to do something you should do.
fact: Useful phrases are «abdicate the throne» and «abdicate responsibility».
exercise: «The king chose to abdicate, so his brother became the new king.» What does abdicate mean? | To become very rich | To build a new castle | To go on a long journey | *To give up being king
exercise: «The queen abdicated after ten years on the throne.» What does abdicated mean here? | To get bored of the food | To travel abroad | To celebrate her birthday | *To give up being queen
exercise: «The manager abdicated his responsibility and went home early.» What does abdicated responsibility suggest? | He won a prize | *He failed to do what he should have done | He asked everyone for help | He did a great job
test: Which phrase means giving up a crown? | Share the cake | *Abdicate the throne | Climb the tower | Paint the fence
test: «Ben would not abdicate his duty to look after the dog.» What does Ben refuse to do? | *Fail to look after the dog | Walk the dog every day | Buy a new dog | Sell the dog
exam: Which sentence uses abdicate correctly? | I abdicated my lunch because it was very tasty | The lazy coach decided to abdicate the team and leave them without a plan | We will abdicate our homework to the rain tonight | *The prince was ready to abdicate the throne when his father retired
